# agent-sandbox environment

## Running commands
{{if .Hook}}- Run shell commands with your normal Bash/Monitor tools; a PreToolUse hook transparently routes each one through the sandbox.
- Command output is returned inline in the tool result.
{{else}}- The Bash and Monitor tools are disabled; use the `run_command` MCP tool to run shell commands.
- Command output is written to files; read the returned stdout/stderr paths.
{{end}}
There is no shell in the loop. The command broker parses your command line
itself (pipelines, `&&`/`||`/`;`, redirections, globbing, `$(…)`, `for`/`if`,
`cd` and the other shell builtins all work), and executes each simple command
directly — never through `bash` or `sh`. The broker never dispatches a
shell, and no shell is in either tier, so nothing the broker runs gives you
a shell directly.

Every command in your line sorts into one of two tiers:

- **Policy commands** run in their own child sandbox, with their own
  filesystem/network grants and argv rules. The broker dispatches to one
  only through nono's own generated shim — never by an absolute path, a
  symlink, or any other indirection that skips it. That guarantee is about
  how the broker itself dispatches; it is not a guarantee about what a
  *different* command's own sandbox can still reach and run — see below.
- **Floor commands** run directly in the broker's own sandbox, with no argv
  rules of their own.

A program in neither tier is never dispatched by the broker — refused
before it starts, the same way an unknown command would be. This allowlist
is absolute about *what the broker itself will dispatch*, not about which
programs can execute: it says nothing about what a command the broker does
dispatch can go on to do once it runs. See the next paragraph.

A toolchain in either tier that can compile and immediately execute code you
give it is bounded only by what *its own* sandbox can reach — not by argv
rules, and not by which other tools are or are not enumerated elsewhere in
this profile — and it can run whatever those grants reach, including a copy
of a program neither tier names, made and executed entirely inside its own
sandbox without ever going through the broker's dispatch or nono's shim. If
such a command is listed below, its own grants (not this document's prose
about tiers) are the real boundary; ask the operator what those are before
assuming a two-tier model holds for everything it might do.

**Policy commands and their denials, from `{{.ProfilePath}}`:**
{{if .PolicyCommands}}{{range .PolicyCommands}}
- `{{.Name}}`{{if .Denials}}, refusing:{{range .Denials}}
  - {{.}}{{end}}{{else}} (no invocations refused){{end}}
{{end -}}
{{else}}
- (none declared in the current profile)
{{end}}
**Floor paths** — every executable inside these (a directory) or the file
itself (a single path) is a floor command, no argv rules, run at the
broker's own grants. This lists *where* they live, not their names — `ls`
the directory (or ask the operator) to see exactly which programs that is:
{{if .FloorPaths}}{{range .FloorPaths}}- `{{.}}`
{{end -}}
{{else}}- (none declared in the current profile)
{{end}}
A refusal always carries a reason on stderr. If a command you need is refused
with no reason and isn't one of the denials above, it is missing from both
tiers, not misbehaving — ask the operator to add it to the command profile,
don't retry it.

One thing the two tiers don't cover: the broker's own shell builtins (`echo`,
`cd`, `test`, `read`, and the others `mvdan.cc/sh` implements) run inside the
broker itself, at the broker's own filesystem grants — they never reach either
tier. A redirect or glob you write is bounded by those same grants, not by
whichever command's output it's attached to.

Which commands may run, what each may touch, and which invocations are
refused is not `agent-sandbox.toml`'s concern — it is the command profile's,
written in nono's own schema, resolved at `{{.ProfilePath}}`.

## Changing the config
The sandbox is configured by `{{.ConfigPath}}`. You may edit it directly.

Editing it **does not affect the current session**: the agent's nono profile is
generated once when the session starts. An edit takes effect the next time the
operator runs `agent-sandbox claude`. The same is true of the command profile
at `{{.ProfilePath}}`: it is read once, when the broker starts.

After editing, run `agent-sandbox ai config-check` — it loads the config and
resolves the agent profile exactly as launch does, so a passing check means the
config will not be what breaks the next launch. `agent-sandbox doctor` checks
the command profile itself: that it exists, that `nono profile validate`
passes on it, that it does not grant write access to the broker's own
binary, that resolving the broker's own name through this process's PATH
lands back on that exact binary, and — when the profile pins the entrypoint's
`executable` — that the pinned path also names that exact binary.

Schema:

```toml
tool_mode = "hook"                    # "hook" | "mcp"

[mcp]
command_output_dir = "..."            # mcp tool_mode only: where command output is written

# Host access for the launched agent — the only profile agent-sandbox
# generates. The sandbox a command runs in is the command profile at the path
# above, configured separately by the operator (see above).
[sandbox.agent]
capabilities = []                     # named bundles; see the list below
allow        = []                     # directories, read+write
read         = []                     # directories, read-only
allow_file   = []                     # single files, read+write
read_file    = []                     # single files, read-only
allow_env    = []                     # environment variable names
```

Capability names: {{range $i, $c := .Capabilities}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$c}}`{{end}}.

A user-scope config at `~/.config/agent-sandbox/config.toml` is composed with
this file: scalars from the project file win, and every list is a de-duplicated
union of the two. So editing the project file can add to a list but cannot
remove what the user-scope file contributes — if a grant you did not write shows
up in `agent-sandbox ai config-check`, it comes from there.
